60,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,Candidate A is home follow-up. B is day rehab. C is perinatal. D is chronic care.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Shion,,A showed real results in the trial. D requires slow data agreements.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,C fits the midwifery slack. B might strain therapy staff who are leaving.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"A is off-site, so no tariff. B needs rooms we don't have. C is on-site.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,A has no data agreement issues. D is risky due to external dependencies.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Kenji, if A earns no tariff, how do we justify it?","{""words"":11,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, the readmission savings might offset the lack of tariff.","{""words"":10,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, that’s a strong argument. C is also strong due to staffing slack.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, C is on-site, so it bills at the standard rate. That’s better for the ledger.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Kenji, but C relies on two departments. The joint clinic failed for that reason.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, that’s a valid point. A is simpler, just nurses and patients.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, A is the most proven. The trial data is clear.","{""words"":11,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I agree. The staffing for A is manageable if we use the slack.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Midori, but the tariff issue still nags me. C is financially safer.","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Kenji, financial safety is good, but patient impact matters too. A has impact.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I vote for A. The readmission reduction is significant.,"{""words"":9,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Shion,,I vote for A. It’s the only one with proven success.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Midori,,I vote for C. The staffing slack makes it feasible and low-risk.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I vote for C. It’s on-site and bills properly.,"{""words"":9,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shizue,,I vote for A. It avoids the joint management pitfalls.,"{""words"":10,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
